They may not be playing optimally, but if they're having fun, that is
all good. (And they can ascend while doing so, too; not optimal doesn't
mean not good.) Still, I don't think NetHack is _designed_ to be played
as an RPG, nor is that the way most people choose to play. (A couple
of wonky personal conducts per player does not an RPG make.) 

> The freefrom gameplay in nethack and countless possible choices at
> every juncture makes it very suitable for roleplay.

Nethack offers trifling rewards for following such "roleplay" guidelines
in terms of alignment bonuses and such. The game doesn't really change
if you roleplay, though; you still have to get the amulet. People
don't talk to you. If you roleplay as outside of your alignment, you get
booted from the Quest. Despite being freeform, everything you have to do
is laid out at the start and immutable.

Honestly, most RPGs work best with _multiple people_, since then there's
someone intelligent changing the game world in response to your actions.
It's very hard for a computer game to do that, and NetHack doesn't
really try. I personally find this a virtue.

